intro:
This track keeps copies of one database on several computers, and asks what goes wrong when the copies drift apart. Lessons start from scenes everyone knows: a café's payments on one computer whose disk dies, a cleanup script that deletes the wrong rows, a phone that shows an old balance after the app said OK, two tills whose lists of stolen cards drift apart while the Wi-Fi is down. Then they open up the machinery, on a real PostgreSQL pair and a real three-computer Cassandra cluster.

By the end, you can ask three questions of any store that keeps copies, and answer them: what does its OK promise, what can a read miss, and what happens to two writes that race? On the way, you'll meet:

- one leader that puts every change in order, followers that copy it, and what a failover can lose;
- the logs that carry changes from the leader to the followers;
- the odd reads a follower that runs behind can give, and how to prevent them;
- PostgreSQL's copies, failover and backups;
- a leader in every region or on every device, and what happens when two of them change the same row;
- stores with no leader at all, which count copies instead;
- how to tell two writes that raced from two that didn't, and data types that merge without losing either.

The eight lessons:

1. One leader, many followers: sync, async and failover
2. Replication logs: statements, WAL and logical rows
3. When followers lag: read-your-writes, monotonic reads and consistent prefix
4. PostgreSQL replication, backups and failover
5. Many leaders: geo-distribution and write conflicts
6. Local-first software and sync engines
7. Leaderless replication: quorums, hinted handoff and read repair
8. Concurrent writes: last write wins, version vectors and CRDTs

You need to read a little code, in Kotlin or Java, and to have Docker on your laptop. Docker is a free program that runs small, separate Linux systems, called containers, on your own computer. The Foundations track's picture of faults and the Storage engines track's write-ahead log (the file a database writes every change to first) help, and each lesson reminds you of what it uses. Agreement between machines (consensus, Raft) and the exact promises a read can make (consistency models) come later, in the Consistency and consensus track.

Most labs model the mechanism on a pretend clock and need only the JDK, the kit that compiles and runs Java and Kotlin, so a minute of replication runs in milliseconds and every test is exact. Lessons 1 to 3, 5 and 6 live in `labs/sim`, lesson 4's in `labs/sql` and lessons 7 and 8's in `labs/nosql`. Run one lesson from `labs/` with `./gradlew :sim:test --tests 'sfz.repl.l05.*'` (each lesson's lab names its module); the finished answers live in `src/solution`. Some Try its use real databases in Docker: a PostgreSQL primary and standby (`env/postgres-ha.yml`) and a three-node Cassandra cluster (`env/cassandra-cluster.yml`, about 3 GB of memory). Start one at a time, and stop it with `down -v` when the lesson ends.
